<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px;">

    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        Lesson Planner – Theme #1 – Lesson # 4
    </h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em>
    </p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Vessels Are Big Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Develop short drafts of texts on class experiments or projects. interactive: Create instructional posters for peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can develop short drafts for projects about the canal by describing facts and current activities (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans. Vessels are crossing it every day.'). Can create simple instructional posters on the Panama Canal for peers (e.g., a colorful illustration of the Panama Canal describing vessels passing through with simple sentences: 'The black vessel is bigger than the white vessel.'). Can edit drafts of project descriptions for clarity (e.g., add another 'n' to the verb 'connects').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276; padding-bottom: 5px; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different vessels (boat, yacht, ship) and the Panama Canal. Students identify the pictures and shout out names. The teacher writes the words on the board, focusing on spelling (e.g., 'canal,' 'vessel,' 'boat').</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "The Panama Canal connects two oceans." and "Many big vessels travel through the canal." The teacher reads the sentences aloud, pointing to each word, emphasizing the /k/ sound in 'connects' and 'canal' and the /b/ sound in 'big' and 'boat' (if mentioned). The teacher then models how to write a similar sentence using different vocabulary, for instance: "A small boat is crossing the water current." or "The big ship is transporting goods." The teacher highlights the present simple and continuous structures.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does the Panama Canal connect?" (Expected: "Two oceans.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are the vessels small or big?" (Expected: "Big.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short factual paragraph about the Panama Canal, displayed on a projector or whiteboard. This text serves as a model for students' later writing.
                        <br>Example Text:
                        <br><em>"The Panama Canal is very important and historic. It connects the Atlantic Ocean and the Pacific Ocean. Many different vessels, like big ships and small yachts, travel through the canal every day. They are transporting goods across the world. Look! A red vessel is crossing the locks right now. It is bigger than the blue boat."</em></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing facts about the Panama Canal and current events.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the text aloud. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected: "The Panama Canal.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students write T or F for the following statements:
                            <br>1. The Panama Canal connects two rivers. (F)
                            <br>2. Ships travel through the canal every day. (T)
                            <br>3. The red vessel is smaller than the blue boat. (F)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using Present Simple, Present Continuous, Comparatives, and target vocabulary (canal, vessel, ship, ocean, connect, travel, big, small).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice (Cloze sentences):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks for students to fill in with appropriate vocabulary words or verb forms based on the previous text. Students write their answers individually.
                            <br>Example:
                            <br>1. The Panama Canal ________ two oceans. (connects)
                            <br>2. Many big ________ travel through the canal. (vessels)
                            <br>3. A vessel ________ the locks now. (is crossing)
                            <br>4. That ship is ________ than this yacht. (bigger)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice (Sentence building):</b> Students receive picture prompts (e.g., a big ship, a small boat, two oceans) and a list of key vocabulary (e.g., 'connect,' 'travel,' 'big,' 'small,' 'ocean,' 'vessel'). They must write one or two sentences for each prompt, ensuring they use the target grammar and vocabulary.
                            <br>Example Prompt: Picture of a big ship in the canal, a small yacht beside it. Words: 'ship,' 'yacht,' 'big,' 'small,' 'travel.'
                            <br>Student writes: "The ship is big. The yacht is small. The ship is bigger than the yacht. They are traveling through the canal."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer check and correction):</b> Students exchange their written sentences from the previous activity with a partner. They read each other's sentences and gently identify any mistakes in spelling (e.g., 'connects' vs 'conects'), grammar, or vocabulary use. The teacher circulates, offering support and clarifying doubts, encouraging respectful conversations about corrections.</li>
                    </ol>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will create a short descriptive text for an instructional poster about the Panama Canal. They should imagine they are explaining the canal to other peers.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual or pair written product.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a blank template (or a piece of paper) with a space for drawing and lines for writing a short paragraph.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to draw an illustration of the Panama Canal, showing vessels, oceans, and/or locks.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, they will write 3-5 sentences describing the canal and what is happening there. They must use at least one sentence with present simple (describing a fact, e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects...'), one with present continuous (describing current activity, e.g., 'Vessels are crossing...'), and one comparative sentence (e.g., 'That ship is bigger than...').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should incorporate vocabulary like 'canal,' 'ocean,' 'vessel,' 'ship,' 'connect,' 'travel,' 'big,' 'small,' 'across,' 'through.'</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence starters provided by the teacher: "The Panama Canal is...", "It connects...", "Today, many vessels are...", "This ship is bigger than..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their short descriptive text/poster drafts from Stage 4. The teacher will review them to check for understanding and application of target structures.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Checklist):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Is the text easy to understand? (Learning outcome: "edit drafts for clarity").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 3-4 target vocabulary words used correctly? (e.g., 'canal,' 'vessel,' 'connects,' 'bigger').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of Present Simple, Present Continuous, and Comparatives (as per A1.2 level).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Structure:</b> Can students produce simple, complete sentences?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Spelling:</b> Basic spelling of familiar words (e.g., 'canal,' 'vessel').</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing you wrote about the Panama Canal today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write sentences about vessels? Why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about writing sentences to describe the Panama Canal?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your writing do you want to improve next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., "Students were engaged by the visual prompts for sentence building.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with spelling 'connects'; I need to provide a word bank or more direct phonics practice for multi-syllable words in the next lesson.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the grammar content (Present Simple, Present Continuous, Comparatives) appropriately integrated for A1.2 writing level? Were the vocabulary words adequately utilized?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration):</b> The short descriptive texts written by students in this lesson will be used as a foundation for a mediation activity in Lesson 5, where students will explain their posters to another group or summarize information from their written work. This will integrate the written output with oral mediation and consolidate learning.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>